[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 500,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 539,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70988,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 539,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Penile adenosquamous carcinomas (ASCs) and mucoepidermoid carcinomas (MECs) are invasive squamous cell carcinomas (SCCs) with glandular and/or mucinous features.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 501,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 540,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70989,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 540,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8560/3 Adenosquamous carcinoma</p>\r\n<p>8430/3 Mucoepidermoid carcinoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 502,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 541,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70990,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 541,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2C81.Y &amp; XH7873 Other specified malignant neoplasm of penis &amp; Adenosquamous carcinoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 503,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 542,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70991,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 542,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 504,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 543,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70992,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 543,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 505,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70993,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These rarely reported tumours are said to affect the glans penis, usually around the urethral meatus. Tumours may also extend to the coronal sulcus and foreskin. They are less common on ventral penile shaft skin {8554104; 27696488}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70994,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients are usually in their sixth decade of life and present with a penile mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70995,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>To date, &lt;&nbsp;15&nbsp;cases have been reported as ASC or MEC {29144557}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70996,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70997,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It is hypothesized that the glandular/mucin-producing tumour cells are from mucinous glands around the meatus. Rarely, metaplastic goblet cells may be present in the foreskin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70998,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are large, firm, erythematous masses involving the glans penis, sometimes with ulceration. They are white-grey and deeply infiltrative, most often replacing the glans penis and sometimes extending to the coronal sulcus and foreskin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70999,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ASCs are considered to be gland-forming SCCs, whereas MECs are solid tumours without overt gland formation. At present, both entities are placed under the rubric of ASC.</p>\r\n<p>The SCC component is predominant, with morphological features of usual-type grade&nbsp;1&ndash;2 SCC. Adjacent penile intraepithelial neoplasia may be present. The glandular component is often in the perimeatal region, with columnar or cuboidal cells, intracellular mucin, and sometimes intraluminal mucin. The glandular component stains for CEA and high-molecular-weight cytokeratin (34&beta;E12) {16924331}.</p>\r\n<p>The term &ldquo;MEC&rdquo; is more appropriate for tumours with only mucin-producing cells, without glandular structures {29144557}. Mucin stains, CK7, EMA (MUC1), MUC5AC, and MUC6 are positive in the mucinous component. CK5, CK14, p40, p16, and p63 are negative in the mucinous cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71000,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 512,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 551,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71001,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 551,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 515,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 554,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71002,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 554,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> SCC with gland formation and/or mucin-producing cells positive for CK7, CEA, and mucin stains and negative for p16.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 513,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 552,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71003,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 552,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Staging follows the same Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) and American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) system as staging of SCC of the penis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 514,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 553,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71004,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 212,
        "headingId": 553,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymph node involvement has been reported in about 50% of patients at the time of diagnosis {25624963}. The mortality rate is reported to be low, but this is based on data from limited follow-up periods. Aggressive behaviour has been reported in some cases of MEC {25624963}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:57:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]